  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nA line/cluster of convection will sag south early this morning \ntoward the Evansville Tri State; however, the storms are expected to \nweaken before or as they move into the CWA. This will be a result of \nthem running into the very warm aloft, associated with the massive \nridge in place. It isn't totally out of the question that a few \nshowers/storms briefly push in before diminishing. \n\nThe aforementioned ridge, responsible for all the heat across \nthe Quad State, will continue to expand and intensify across the\narea into the weekend, which will allow temperatures to climb \nalong with the humidity. The Extreme Heat Warning will remain in\nplace and unchanged with heat index values expected to be in \nthe 105-115 degree range through the weekend! This will be a \ndangerous and prolonged period of extreme heat.\n\nA frontal boundary and several pieces of upper-level energy are \nforecast to drift through the area for Monday, which may briefly \nbring slightly cooler temperatures (upper 80s to low 90s) for \nTuesday into Wednesday of next week. Beyond that, there are some \nsignals that temperatures will trend a bit cooler with additional \nrainfall chances toward the end of next week, even though \ntemperatures may briefly rebound Wednesday into Thursday.",
